/-!
# Certificate: the closed formula  Ob(λ) = -det C · λ(λ-1)  at explicit non-Fermat cubic sixfolds

Lean 4 core only: no Mathlib, no `native_decide`, no `sorry`.  GENERATED by
`night/lean_ob/gen_closedformula.py` from `ClosedFormulaCert.template.lean`.  The numbers are those of
the audit's exact computation `night/audit/exact4.py` (run unchanged through `exact4_dump.py`); the
generator recomputes everything with a Python mirror of the definitions below (`cf4.py`).
Theory: `night/PROOF_A.md`, sections 0, 2 and 8.1.

## Setting and conventions (those of exact4.py)

X = {F = 0} ⊂ P⁷ a cubic sixfold containing P₁ = {x₀=x₁=x₂=x₃=0} and P₂ = {x₃=x₄=x₅=x₆=0}, which meet in
q = e₇:      F = x₃·Q + Σ_{i∈{0,1,2}, j∈{4,5,6}} xᵢ xⱼ l_ij,     C = (l_ij(q)),  Q(q) = coefficient of x₇² in Q.
 * On P₁, plane coordinates (y₀,y₁,y₂,y₃) = (x₄,x₅,x₆,x₇); regular sequence, in this order,
   (q₀,q₁,q₂,q₃) = (g₀,g₁,g₂,Q)|P₁ with gᵢ = Σ_j xⱼ l_ij; the "special" member (paired with x₃) is q₃.
 * On P₂, plane coordinates (y₀,y₁,y₂,y₃) = (x₀,x₁,x₂,x₇); regular sequence, in this order,
   (Q, g'₄, g'₅, g'₆)|P₂ with g'ⱼ = Σ_i xᵢ l_ij; the special member is the first one.
 * τ = the functional on quartics that vanishes on the ideal (q)₄ and has τ(Jac) = 16, where
   Jac = det(∂qᵢ/∂y_k) with ROWS IN THE ORDER OF THE SEQUENCE.  (Classically τ(h) = Res[h dy/(q)].)
   On P₂ the order (Q, g'₄, g'₅, g'₆) differs from the order (g'₄, g'₅, g'₆, Q) of PROOF_A §8.1 by a
   cyclic permutation of sign -1; so τ' here is MINUS the residue of §8.1.  This is the sign `sgn = -1`
   in the P₂ statements, and it is the reason why c₁ = +det C while c₂ = -det C.
 * R = the squared residue on sextics, R(h) = Res[h dy/(…, q_special², …)], characterised by
   R(qᵢ·h') = 0 for the three non-special i and R(q_special·h') = τ(h') for all quartics h'.
 * γ = a cubic vanishing at q (no y₃³ term) with τ(y_k·γ) = δ_{k3}; the audit's excess direction
   restricted to the plane.  c₂ = R(γ²) on P₁, c₁ = R'(γ'²) on P₂, and the claim is
   c₂ = -det C, c₁ = +det C, so that  c₂λ² + c₁λ = -det C·λ(λ-1).
 * det Λ = det(l_ij|P), a cubic in the plane coordinates.
Denominators are cleared: τ(m) = tauN(m)/tauD, R(m) = RN(m)/RD, γ = gamN/gamD (tauD, RD, gamD ≠ 0).

## What the Lean kernel checks, for each of the cubics below and each of its two planes

 * `…_wf`      the tables tauN, RN list exactly the 35 quartic / 84 sextic monomials; the denominators
               are nonzero; γ is a cubic; the four qᵢ are quadrics.
 * `…_tau_kills`  tauN vanishes on qᵢ·m for the 4 × 10 products (i = 0..3, m a quadric monomial);
   `…_tau_jac`    tauN(Jac(q)) = 16·tauD  (assembled from the four terms `…_jac0..3` of the expansion
                  of the determinant along its first row, by linearity of `ev`: `tauJac_of`).
 * `…_trans_law`  tauN(y_k·det Λ) = 0 for k = 0,1,2 and tauN(y₃·det Λ)·Q(q) = sgn·tauD
                  (i.e. τ(x₇ det Λ) = ±1/Q(q): the transformation law of §8.1, verified, not assumed).
 * `…_R_kills`, `…_R_special`  RN vanishes on qᵢ·m for the three non-special i and all 35 quartic
                  monomials m, and RN(q_special·m)/RD = tauN(m)/tauD;
   `…_R_detLam`   RN(det Λ²)·Q(q)² = sgn·det C·RD   (§8.1: Res[det Λ² dx/(q₀,q₁,q₂,q₃²)] = det C/Q(q)²).
 * `…_gamma`      γ has no y₃³ term, tauN(y_k·gamN) = 0 for k = 0,1,2 and tauN(y₃·gamN) = tauD·gamD.
 * `…_c2_residue`, `…_c1_residue`   RN(gamN²) = c·gamD²·RD with the integer c printed by exact4.py;
   `…_c2_eq`, `…_c1_eq`   c₂ = -det C,  c₁ = +det C;   `…_detC`, `…_Qq`  the values of det C and Q(q);
   `…_closed_formula`, `…_closed_formula_rat`   c₂·λ² + c₁·λ = -det C·λ·(λ-1) for every integer
                  (rational) λ.
The cubics: `A` = audit/cubic_F.json (det C = -1), `B` = audit/cubic_F_seed8.json (det C = 2),
`Dg` = audit/cubic_F_seed4_degenerate.json (det C = 0: the obstruction vanishes identically,
`Dg_obstruction_zero`).

## NOT formalized

 * The theory: that τ, R are residues with the stated properties, that the Hodge-locus obstruction of
   [P₁] + λ[P₂] is c₂λ² + c₁λ in the audit's normalisation (PROOF_A §§2–4, 8; Griffiths–Dwork etc.).
 * Uniqueness of the tables: that the equations `tau_kills` + `tau_jac`, resp. `R_kills` +
   `R_special`, have only one solution.  This is the classical statement dim (q)₄ = 34 and (q)₆ = A₆
   for a regular sequence of four quadrics; the generator checks both ranks (34 and 84) exactly in
   Python for every plane, but no rank certificate is given to the kernel.
 * Smoothness of the three cubics (the audit's job), and the restriction formulas' geometric meaning.

Monomial codes: y₀^a y₁^b y₂^c y₃^d ↦ a + 8b + 64c + 512d (octal digits; all degrees ≤ 6).
